{"author":{"id":"673e1e1b6fccff4da5524407c8a8cdc7caf18221aabb93f2bb36ffef5c2a95e8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1781331243,"nonce":"M9cKslNvRnfExUvosuOg/GBUeEE=","metadata":{"github-id":"I_kwDOSn4-Gc8AAAABFW8oMw","github-url":"https://github.com/kanya-approve/karpenter-provider-rackspace-spot/issues/14","origin":"github"},"title":"Node overhead (KubeReserved) is flat 100m/100Mi regardless of instance size","message":"`translate()` assigns the same KubeReserved to every ServerClass, with no scaling by instance size and no SystemReserved or eviction threshold.\n\n### Evidence\n`pkg/providers/instancetype/instancetype.go:159-170`:\n```go\nOverhead: \u0026karpcloudprovider.InstanceTypeOverhead{\n    KubeReserved: corev1.ResourceList{\n        corev1.ResourceCPU:    resource.MustParse(\"100m\"),\n        corev1.ResourceMemory: resource.MustParse(\"100Mi\"),\n    },\n},\n```\n\n### Impact\nOn large ServerClasses this drastically understates reserved resources, so reported **Allocatable is overstated**. Karpenter packs pods against an inflated Allocatable → node CPU/memory pressure, evictions, and failed schedulings on big nodes. (AWS scales kube-reserved with instance size; kubelet defaults also scale.)\n\n### Suggested fix\n- Scale KubeReserved by node size (a tiered/formulaic model akin to GKE/EKS reserved curves), keyed on the ServerClass CPU/mem.\n- Add SystemReserved and an eviction-hard threshold, or make overhead configurable on the NodeClass.\n- Unit-test the reserved curve at small/medium/large sizes.","files":null}]}